WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.540
Changes in your diet not
only affect you physically,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.520
physiologically inside,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.280
but also mentally,
how well you think,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.800
psychologically,
how well you feel.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.860
But you’ll never know just
how good you can feel

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.720
until you put it to the test
and try eating healthy.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.000
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.700
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.140
Today we look at the relationship
between exercise and weight loss.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.460
And we start with a question:

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.840
can exercising in cool water
undermine weight loss

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.140
by leading to an increase in
post workout calorie intake?

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.700
Swimming and aquatic exercise
in general, are popular alternatives

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.460
to land-based activities
such as walking or biking.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.080
The buoyancy helps take some

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.880
of the weight-bearing stress
off of joints,

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.140
but swimming appears to be
less effective for weight loss.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.620
Obese women were randomized
into an hour a day

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.380
of walking, cycling, or swimming.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.100
Six months later the walkers
lost an average of 17 pounds,

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.220
the cyclists lost
an average of 19 pounds,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.580
and the swimmers
didn’t lose an ounce

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.160
(in fact, they actually
gained 5 pounds).

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.620
Gauging skin folds
to estimate body fat,

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.020
the measurements slimmed
more than 40 percent

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.760
in the walking
and cycling groups,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.580
but there was no change at all
in the swimming group.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.340
What’s going on?
And check this out.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.680
The more the women walked,
the more they lost weight.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.620
The more the women biked,
the more they lost weight.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.800
But the more laps they did
didn’t seem to matter.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.500
Even an hour a day.
No weight loss.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.440
What is going on?

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.780
Well, it turns out
that some exercise

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.760
boosts appetite
more than others.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.940
While land-based exercise
does not stimulate

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.760
a compensatory increase
in appetite and calorie intake,

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.940
the same cannot be said
for water-based exercise.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:59.280
In contrast to walking,
in contrast to running,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.320
and in contrast
to cycling,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.640
swimming can significantly
heighten hunger within hours.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.760
This may explain why swimmers
tend to have more body fat

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.460
than runners of equal caliber,
even though they may be

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.520
actually expending more
calories during training.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.860
If anything, you’d think
swimming might lead

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.980
to even greater weight loss since
you’re losing heat to the water.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.800
But swimming didn’t
seem to work at all.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:28.020
The cold, it turns out,
may actually be the culprit.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:32.340
If you exercise
in warm water (about 90o F)

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.340
it does not boost your appetite
more than exercising on land.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.700
After the same workout
in cool water (about 70° F),

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.580
people can end up eating
more than twice as much

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.600
at a meal an hour later.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.500
Maybe they’re just burning off
extra calories to stay warm?

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.800
No, even at the same number
of calories expended,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.480
people eat hundreds of calories
more after exercising in cold water.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.140
Offered a buffet after burning off
about 500 calories in cool water,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.400
people eat nearly 900 calories,
hundreds more than after exercising

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.500
in warm water
or just staying dry.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.440
So, they ended up taking
in about twice as many

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:08.940
calories as they exercised off.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.480
No wonder swimming doesn’t
appear useful for weight loss.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.160
Would the same
thing happen

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.200
under different
temperatures on land?

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.500
A team of British researchers
sought to find out,

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.540
randomizing people
to briskly walk

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.440
for 45 minutes on a treadmill
in the cold (about 46° F)

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.620
or at closer to room
temperature (about 68° F).

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.440
Participants were then
presented with a buffet meal

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.480
in which their eating
was covertly recorded.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.600
And calorie intake
was significantly greater

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.860
after exercising in the cold.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.280
Though walking is often prescribed
for overweight individuals,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.160
the researchers conclude,
“if walking was to take place

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.900
in a cold environment, such as in winter,
then this may stimulate food intake.”

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.080
In the warmer months,
though, obesity researchers

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.600
suggest exercising outdoors
may be preferable

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.520
to an air-conditioned gym.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.980
All studies to date on the effects
of hot and cold environments

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.920
have found that exercising
in cool water or under cool conditions

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.700
on land led to an increase
in post-workout calorie intake.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.420
What about a quick dip
in the pool after you exercise?

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.600
Australian researchers found
that immersion in water

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.660
for 15 minutes—cool or warm—
after a running session

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.600
resulted in increased
calorie intake.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.020
What is it about getting
wet that whets your appetite?

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.240
Maybe they got
a chill after getting out

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.040
before they could change
into dry clothes?

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.800
This suggests that though
a cool shower after a workout

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.100
may be invigorating, it might
be better to stick to hot.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.980
Next up, we look at what role inactivity
has played in the obesity epidemic.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.160
Right now, almost 2/3 of
Americans are overweight.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.220
And by 2030, more than
1/2 our population

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.840
may be clinically obese.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.300
Childhood obesity
has tripled

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.000
and most of them will grow up
to be overweight as well.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.000
The United States may be
in the midst of raising

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.280
the first generation since
our nation's founding

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.920
that will have a shorter
predicted life span than that

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:03.300
of the previous
generation.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.640
The food industry
blames...

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.140
inactivity.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.580
You just need
to move more.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.660
But what is the role of exercise
in the treatment of obesity?

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.840
There's considerable debate
in the medical literature today

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.840
about whether physical activity
has any role whatsoever

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.160
in the epidemic of obesity that's
swept the globe since the 80s.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.900
The increase in
calories per person

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.980
is more than sufficient to explain
the U.S. epidemic of obesity.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.860
In fact, if anything, the
level of physical activity

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.080
over the last few decades
has actually gone up

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.580
in both Europe and
North America.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.760
This has important
policy implications.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.860
Yes, we still need
to exercise more,

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.280
but the priorities for reversing
the obesity epidemic

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.860
should focus on the
overconsumption of calories.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.580
To work off the increased
caloric intake,

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:54.360
which for kids it's like an extra
can of soda and small fries,

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.460
compared to what they were
eating back in the 70s,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.500
and for adults it's like
an extra Big Mac a day,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:04.080
to work that off you'd have to
walk 2 hours a day, 7 days a week.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.620
So exercise can prevent weight
gain, but the amount required

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:11.100
to prevent weight gain may be closer
to twice the current recommendations.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.920
Public health advocates
have been experimenting

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.660
with including this
kind of information.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.240
The fast-food menu
labeled with calories

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.960
and the number of miles to
walk to burn those calories

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.180
appeared to be most
effective in influencing

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.880
the selection of
lower calorie meals.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:30.300
Now exercise alone may
have a small effect,

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:33.960
and that small effect can make a big
difference on the population scale.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.800
A 1% decrease in BMI
nationwide might prevent

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.260
millions of cases of diabetes
and heart disease,

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:41.880
thousands of
cases of cancer...

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:46.320
But why don't we lose more
weight from exercise?

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:49.980
Maybe because we're just
not doing it enough.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.860
The small magnitude of
weight loss observed

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:54.600
from the majority of
exercise interventions,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:55.860
you know where they
make people exercise,

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.280
may be primarily due to
how low the doses are

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:00.540
of the prescribed
exercise.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.460
People tend to over estimate
how many calories are burned

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.660
by physical
activity.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:11.940
For example, there's this myth
that a bout of sexual activity

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.200
burns a few
hundred calories.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.140
So, hey, you could get a
side of fries with that.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.780
But if you actually
hook people up

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:22.260
and measure energy
expenditure during the act

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.580
and your study subjects don't get too
tangled up with all the wires and hoses,

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:31.080
though it may be merely the metabolic
equivalent of calisthenics,

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:36.600
given that the average bout of sexual
activity only lasts about 6 minutes,

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:42.060
a young man might expend approximately
21 calories during sexual intercourse.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.780
Of course, he would've spent
roughly 1/3 of that

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:47.040
just lying around
watching t.v.–

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:48.480
just basal
metabolism.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.580
So the incremental benefit is plausibly
on the order of 14 calories.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.780
So maybe we could have,
like, 1 fry with that.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.900
Finally today,
what’s more important:

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.360
diet or exercise when it
comes to weight loss.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.460
You may be surprised
at the answer.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:09.480
When trying to lose weight,
which is most important:

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:10.740
diet or exercise?

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:13.920
This is what a survey
found recently:

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:17.940
“The vast majority of those trying to
lose or maintain weight believe that

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:21.900
both monitoring food and beverage
consumption and physical activity

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.380
are equally important in weight
management and weight loss.”

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:30.300
Most people go with equally important,
and then exercise, and then diet.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.000
And, most people are wrong.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.020
Identified as one of the
most common misconceptions

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:39.120
about obesity in
this recent review,

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.200
the “Confusion about the leverage
of exercise on body weight.”

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.140
“Unfortunately, the
energy balance equation

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.080
[you know, calories in
have to equal calories out]

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:54.060
suggests that energy intake and energy
expenditure occupy equivalent roles

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:58.680
in determining energy balance,
when in fact the factors governing

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:02.520
energy intakes influence the
energy balance far more powerfully

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.840
than the factors determining
resting energy expenditure.”

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.840
What we put in our mouths
is most important.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:14.640
For example, to walk off the calories
found in single pat of butter,

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.380
you’d have to add an extra 700
yards to your stroll that evening.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:25.140
A quarter-mile jog for each
sardine we put in our mouth—

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.060
and that’s just the edible part.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:30.300
And those who choose
to eat two chicken legs

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.100
better get out on
their own two legs,

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:36.180
and run an extra three miles
that day to outrun weight gain.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:41.507
And that’s for steamed chicken;
skin removed.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.820
